Post-Surgical Pain (Acute)
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Localized incisional pain, Pain exacerbated by movement/coughing, Progressive daily improvement

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-047
Reason for Visit: Severe, acute exacerbation of post-operative incisional pain causing sudden inability to mobilize, breathe deeply, or transfer out of bed.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient 3 days post-abdominal surgery experiencing a severe spike in localized wound pain and an absolute refusal to move or cough. Urgent assessment was critical to rule out acute wound dehiscence, severe surgical site infection, or a deep hematoma, and to administer immediate, potent analgesia to prevent dangerous respiratory complications (atelectasis) and deep vein thrombosis due to immobility prior to morning hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My incision feels like it is tearing open every time I try to move or take a breath; the pain is so sharp I can't even sit up to go to the toilet."
History of Present Illness: Patient is 3 days post-uncomplicated open appendectomy, discharged yesterday. Presents with an acute exacerbation of somatic pain strictly confined to the immediate anatomical site of the surgical incision. Timeline: Pain was well-controlled on discharge but escalated sharply 6 hours ago when the patient attempted to transition from the bed to the bathroom without using a supportive splinting technique. The pain is consistently described as sharp, stabbing, and deeply throbbing. There is an acute exacerbation of pain reliably provoked by any movement, coughing, sneezing, or deep breathing. Patient exhibits distinct protective guarding, severe muscle spasm, and an extreme reluctance to ambulate due to anticipated pain.
Functional Status: Acutely bedbound; completely refusing to perform required post-operative mobilization.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of post-operative complications (pneumonia, DVT) secondary to absolute pain-induced immobility and shallow breathing.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 146/88 | HR: 102 | Temp: 37.2 | RR: 22 (Shallow) | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Alert, diaphoretic, in visible distress, lying rigidly supine and taking deliberately shallow breaths.
Physical Exam: Inspection of the right lower quadrant surgical wound reveals intact sutures/staples with absolutely no signs of wound dehiscence. There is expected mild localized edema and faint erythema directly bordering the incision line, but no spreading cellulitis, gross purulent discharge, or fluctuant mass to suggest a deep hematoma or abscess. Localized point tenderness is present directly over the incision. Abdomen is soft and non-distended away from the surgical site; bowel sounds are present. Respiratory exam reveals shallow expansion bilaterally due to pain guarding, but clear lung fields. Transient tachycardia (HR 102) correlates directly with the nociceptive drive of the pain spikes.
Environmental Observations: Standard bed height makes unassisted transfers highly painful.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking prescribed Paracetamol and Ibuprofen. Has only taken one dose of prescribed Endone (Oxycodone) 5mg yesterday due to fear of addiction.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute, severe, unmanaged Post-Surgical Somatic Pain - Status: Acute
Differentials:

Wound Dehiscence / Evisceration - Ruled out completely by direct visual inspection confirming fully intact, opposed wound edges and absence of protruding tissue or sudden massive fluid leakage.

Surgical Site Infection (SSI) / Abscess - Ruled out clinically by the lack of high fever, absence of spreading fiery redness, no purulent discharge, and the normal, soft feel of the surrounding abdomen.

Post-Operative Pneumonia - Ruled out currently by clear lung fields and lack of productive cough or fever, but patient is at extremely high risk due to shallow breathing.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a severe, immobilizing pain crisis three days post-operatively. The critical clinical imperative was to inspect the wound to definitively exclude catastrophic surgical failure (dehiscence, severe infection, internal bleeding). Direct examination confirmed a clean, intact, normally healing wound, establishing that the crisis was driven by severe, unmanaged, expected nociceptive pain due to under-dosing of prescribed breakthrough analgesia. The patient's absolute refusal to move or breathe deeply posed a significant, immediate threat of pneumonia and DVT. Urgent after-hours assessment safely ruled out surgical emergencies, provided vital reassurance, and facilitated the immediate, supervised administration of short-acting systemic analgesics to break the pain-guarding cycle and restore essential post-operative lung expansion and mobility.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral Oxycodone (Endone) 10mg STAT (from the patient's existing but unused supply). Instructed the patient to take the prescribed Endone 5mg QID strictly for the next 48 hours to establish a baseline analgesic blanket, alongside regular Paracetamol and Ibuprofen.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Hold a firm, rolled-up towel tightly over the incision with both hands whenever attempting to stand, cough, or take a deep breath. Consequence: The towel physically splints the abdominal wall, taking the mechanical tension off the stitches and preventing the sudden, agonizing "tearing" pain spikes (negative reinforcement).
Action: Take the strong breakthrough pain medication exactly 45 minutes before attempting a required walk down the hallway. Consequence: Achieving a pre-emptive analgesic blanket, making the essential physical therapy tolerable and significantly less traumatic (positive reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): N/A
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A
Follow-up: Routine surgical outpatient follow-up in 7-10 days for staple/suture removal.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if there is a sudden gush of fluid or blood from the wound, if the wound edges physically pull apart, or if the patient develops a high fever with shivering.

PATIENT INFORMATION

